WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
Do adults learn languages like children?

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.280
This is a subject that comes
up all the time, and I'm

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.720
going to give you my take on it.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.020
I'm going to look at it from
four different points of view.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.020
First, I'm going to talk about
how the brain works, whether

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.790
it be in adults and children.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.800
Second of all, I'm going to talk about the

00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:19.945
tremendous flexibility that the child has.

00:00:20.035 --> 00:00:25.284
The third is the previous knowledge,
or the accumulated knowledge and

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.205
experience, that the adult has.

00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:30.865
And finally, I'm going to talk about
the difference in attitude between

00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:34.254
a child learning a language, and
an adult learning a language.

00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:36.435
Now, I'm not a neuroscientist.

00:00:36.464 --> 00:00:41.835
I'm sort of distilling for you what
I have read in books by Manfred Spitzer,

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:45.135
whom I mention quite frequently,
and also an audio book that I've been

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:52.575
listening to called "Wie Erwachsene Denken und Lernen", which traces how adults think and learn.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.960
And traces the evolution of our brains
from a neuro-scientific mapping of the

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.630
brain perspective, and a few other sources.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.110
The first thing to realize is
that our brains do not just

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.880
exist for learning languages.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:12.420
In fact, the brain is a learning machine
in the sense that the brain has to acquire

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.630
the knowledge, the ability to anticipate,

00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:17.845
in order for us to survive.

00:01:17.935 --> 00:01:21.685
So it develops knowledge based on input.

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:25.885
It learns that if I touch something hot,
then I've gotta withdraw my hand right

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:28.135
away because it's painful at some point.

00:01:28.225 --> 00:01:33.295
Even in an animal, if it sees a predator,
it's gotta know that the response is to

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:35.065
get out of there as quickly as possible.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:39.025
So the brain develops
an ability to respond.

00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:43.785
To input with output, with an action,
with speech, or with something.

00:01:43.815 --> 00:01:49.244
And this is done, as I described in a
previous video, by these inputs going

00:01:49.244 --> 00:01:53.294
to the hippocampus where they are
stored, on a short-term basis, and

00:01:53.294 --> 00:01:56.505
then they're passed on to the cortex.

00:01:56.625 --> 00:01:59.865
And there's different areas of the
cortex where, gradually, a longer

00:01:59.865 --> 00:02:02.595
term memory is developed longer term.

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:08.275
Ability, call it explicit
knowledge, is stored, which enables

00:02:08.275 --> 00:02:10.555
us to function as human beings.

00:02:10.615 --> 00:02:16.645
We know that if we hear or see or speak a
word, there is a corresponding place for

00:02:16.645 --> 00:02:22.345
that in our brains, neurons, and that
neurons that are adjacent to each other,

00:02:22.345 --> 00:02:28.075
similar to each other because the neurons
are stored in areas of like by category.

00:02:28.075 --> 00:02:29.245
And if there is enough

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.079
stimulus and enough similar neurons
nearby, they may eventually fire

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:36.760
and connect through synapses.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.040
And that's how learning takes place.

00:02:39.045 --> 00:02:44.019
So this process is the same whether
we're talking about a baby or an adult.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.670
So now let's look at the baby.

00:02:45.795 --> 00:02:51.435
So the baby has what, again,
Spitzer refers to as "unlimited

00:02:51.435 --> 00:02:54.225
potential and very limited capability."

00:02:54.285 --> 00:02:58.275
And if we just take the question
of sounds, English has 44.

00:02:58.335 --> 00:02:59.895
Japanese has a very small number.

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:04.275
Spanish has also not such a large number
because those languages have fewer vowels.

00:03:04.485 --> 00:03:09.125
Danish, which has this enormous number
of vowels, has an enormous number of phonemes.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.050
But the baby can learn to be a
native speaker in any of them.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.850
It has total potential, limited
capability. And what's more, apparently

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.080
babies like new sounds, so they're
very much interested in receiving

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.090
and imitating these new sounds.

00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:27.704
So that the brain of the
child is very flexible.

00:03:27.765 --> 00:03:31.365
It can develop in any particular
way, depending on the input that

00:03:31.365 --> 00:03:33.045
it receives from its environment.

00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:36.765
In fact, the child even
starts hearing language in the

00:03:36.765 --> 00:03:38.115
womb, and that's been proven.

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:39.105
So that's the baby.

00:03:39.105 --> 00:03:44.144
Very flexible, limited impulse
control, interested in new things.

00:03:44.415 --> 00:03:49.545
Imitates and has no anxiety about
anything, and that's the baby.

00:03:49.665 --> 00:03:55.155
So the process whereby the baby
learns the language is similar

00:03:55.155 --> 00:03:56.805
to how adults learn the language.

00:03:56.955 --> 00:04:01.725
I mentioned previously, for example,
that in the past tense of verbs in

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.285
English, a young child, two years
old, will first use the form of the

00:04:06.285 --> 00:04:09.855
irregular verbs in English correctly,
the very common irregular verbs.

00:04:09.945 --> 00:04:10.605
Then as it

00:04:10.635 --> 00:04:13.995
gets a sense of the rule that
the past tense is created by -ed.

00:04:14.505 --> 00:04:19.005
It'll then use the past tense of regular
verbs correctly, and then at a later

00:04:19.005 --> 00:04:23.265
stage, it'll learn the past tense of
the less frequent irregular verbs.

00:04:23.355 --> 00:04:24.945
And the same happens in German.

00:04:25.155 --> 00:04:27.765
And the same happens
when adults learn verbs.

00:04:27.825 --> 00:04:32.445
So the message here is that the brain
starts to create rules for itself.

00:04:32.474 --> 00:04:36.795
If the baby were instructed in
its native language, the way so

00:04:36.795 --> 00:04:40.365
many of us are instructed in the
languages we're trying to learn.

00:04:40.665 --> 00:04:43.515
The baby would probably never
learn to speak that language.

00:04:43.605 --> 00:04:46.815
In other words, the natural
process is more efficient.

00:04:47.025 --> 00:04:52.575
The natural process of allowing the
input to bombard the brain with enough

00:04:52.575 --> 00:04:56.955
frequency, with enough similar words,
the brain starts to form these rules.

00:04:56.955 --> 00:05:01.065
There are even apparently
neural locations for rules that

00:05:01.235 --> 00:05:03.395
are developed through this
process of listening and reading.

00:05:03.485 --> 00:05:04.655
So that's common [between adults and babies].

00:05:04.745 --> 00:05:06.095
Now let's look at what's different.

00:05:06.275 --> 00:05:08.255
A baby has no prior knowledge of anything.

00:05:08.285 --> 00:05:12.125
Everything is new, so the brain
of the baby is a blank slate.

00:05:12.335 --> 00:05:15.605
You can imprint anything you
want on the baby's brain.

00:05:15.725 --> 00:05:21.245
An adult has prior knowledge, so
that can be good and that can be bad.

00:05:21.245 --> 00:05:22.385
As I'm going to explain.

00:05:22.565 --> 00:05:30.275
First of all, the less favorable situation
is that the mind of the adult has already

00:05:30.469 --> 00:05:35.300
hardened around a certain range
of sounds, for example, or certain

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.219
patterns of how to express things.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.770
It is no longer as flexible as the baby.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.870
It's not open to any influence.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.190
It has its own native language,
which makes it more difficult now

00:05:46.195 --> 00:05:51.950
to imprint or to introduce new ways
of saying things and new sounds.

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:57.230
However, on the other hand, the
adult has knowledge. So when the baby

00:05:57.289 --> 00:05:59.120
sees a tree, or a house, or a car,

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.680
it doesn't know what a tree or a house or
a car is, at first, but the adult does.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.330
The adult knows things.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.070
It knows what objects are.

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:13.020
The adult even knows how, at least
his own language or her own language,

00:06:13.025 --> 00:06:16.469
works. The adult knows intrinsically,

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:20.010
even without consulting a
grammar book, what a noun is,

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.960
what a verb is. And when the adult sees or
hears words in a new language, they start

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:31.479
to get a sense, even without the grammar
explanation, of the different functions of

00:06:31.539 --> 00:06:37.000
words in a sentence. The adult can read.
The adult can read a grammar explanation.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:41.320
I've said before, the grammar
explanation will not by itself enable

00:06:41.505 --> 00:06:45.075
the adult to perform
according to those rules.

00:06:45.224 --> 00:06:50.625
However, it may help the adult notice
what is happening in the new language.

00:06:50.685 --> 00:06:54.525
So that can be an assist to the adult,
which is not available to the child.

00:06:54.555 --> 00:07:00.195
And when we get to more complex concepts
like emotions or abstract words,

00:07:00.765 --> 00:07:04.755
the adult knows these words because
the adult has a rich vocabulary

00:07:04.755 --> 00:07:09.075
in his or her own language. And
therefore can very quickly acquire

00:07:09.135 --> 00:07:12.435
the same corresponding words in a
new language. The adult can read.

00:07:12.525 --> 00:07:16.995
Reading is a phenomenal way of increasing
our ability in a foreign language.

00:07:17.085 --> 00:07:20.445
We know that when we are reading, it's
as if we are speaking the language,

00:07:20.445 --> 00:07:21.945
insofar as the brain is concerned.

00:07:22.034 --> 00:07:26.335
If we do a lot of reading, We can
actually condition our brains to get

00:07:26.335 --> 00:07:31.375
used to the patterns of the new language,
to develop patterns in the brain that

00:07:31.375 --> 00:07:33.325
help us deal with this new language.

00:07:33.354 --> 00:07:37.255
Adults who read well in their own
language typically will be able to read

00:07:37.255 --> 00:07:39.565
well eventually in the new language.

00:07:39.594 --> 00:07:42.625
So these are advantages
that the adult has.

00:07:42.715 --> 00:07:45.474
The adult can download a
podcast and listen to it.

00:07:45.474 --> 00:07:46.674
The baby's not going to do that.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:51.984
So that the adult has learning strategies
that the baby obviously doesn't have.

00:07:51.984 --> 00:07:56.635
The baby responds to impulses and
is not sort of strategic in its

00:07:56.635 --> 00:07:58.044
approach to learning a language.

00:07:58.135 --> 00:07:59.695
So that's the adult.

00:07:59.755 --> 00:08:04.434
Now, one of the things that 
Spitzer and Herschkowitz explain in this

00:08:04.434 --> 00:08:06.114
audio book that I'm listening to,

00:08:08.215 --> 00:08:12.985
is that our prefrontal cortex,
which controls our impulses, sort

00:08:12.985 --> 00:08:16.795
of our developing sense of values
that help us have a better sense as

00:08:16.795 --> 00:08:19.075
adults of what's good and bad for us.

00:08:19.135 --> 00:08:24.085
This evaluation ability to evaluate,
the ability to develop a strategy,

00:08:24.085 --> 00:08:27.895
all of these things take place
in the prefrontal cortex, which

00:08:27.985 --> 00:08:32.545
reaches its kind of fullest stage of
development, in a way, at the age of 25.

00:08:32.605 --> 00:08:37.555
So by that time, the adult is better
able, A) to control its impulses,

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:41.930
but B), to elaborate or to develop
a strategy based on its own

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:43.790
values. That's going to help it learn.

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:47.390
So those are things that are happening
and actually continue to happen.

00:08:47.395 --> 00:08:51.380
Apparently as time goes on, the evolution
in the brain is such that the brain

00:08:51.385 --> 00:08:56.270
gets better connected, the emotions and
the thinking gets all better connected

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:57.680
in the brain, so that we end up,

00:08:57.925 --> 00:09:02.364
as we grow older, somewhat more
balanced in how we evaluate things

00:09:02.364 --> 00:09:03.864
and in our approach to life.

00:09:03.925 --> 00:09:08.454
So we've now covered that the brain
essentially functions the same, that the

00:09:08.454 --> 00:09:13.584
child has by dint of being a blank slate,
a greater potential, although more limited

00:09:13.584 --> 00:09:16.734
capabilities. The adult has more limited

00:09:16.755 --> 00:09:22.275
potential perhaps, but has capabilities
that can compensate for that. And also

00:09:22.275 --> 00:09:25.335
through reading and other strategies,
can acquire a vast vocabulary.

00:09:25.365 --> 00:09:28.755
But may have more difficulty
pronouncing as well as a child.

00:09:28.905 --> 00:09:32.235
So now we come to the fourth thing,
and that is the evolution of attitude.

00:09:32.295 --> 00:09:36.645
Research has demonstrated that the child
is very much attracted to new things.

00:09:36.675 --> 00:09:38.115
New is good for the child.

00:09:38.175 --> 00:09:38.595
"Wow!

00:09:38.595 --> 00:09:39.045
Look at that!

00:09:39.045 --> 00:09:39.555
Look at this!"

00:09:39.615 --> 00:09:45.975
As the adult gets older, he or she
becomes more set in their ways.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:49.870
In other words, the adult may
consider something new that has to

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.970
be learned as something undesirable.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:55.660
I always remember when I started
learning Chinese, there was another

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:59.320
Canadian diplomat there learning with
me, and when he heard that in Mandarin

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.270
Chinese to say, "Are you going?" we
actually say 你去不去？, "You go not go?"

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.910
And his response to that
was, "Is that ever stupid!"

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:11.350
Now, if you consider the new language to
be stupid or inconvenient, or you don't

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:17.010
like it, or any attitude that is negative
towards the new, you're not going to learn.

00:10:17.069 --> 00:10:20.969
The baby tends not to have any of
these negative thoughts towards things

00:10:20.969 --> 00:10:25.410
new, whereas often adults, perhaps
because of their own languages,

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.730
tied up with their identity, or
they're just reluctant to change.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.240
And it also depends on whether
you are a positive person,

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.189
whether you think you can succeed.

00:10:35.699 --> 00:10:38.100
The child doesn't worry about
"Am I going to learn this language?"

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.360
It's just... the language comes in.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.829
Furthermore, whether the adult has already
learned languages, it's pointed out in

00:10:43.835 --> 00:10:48.060
Spitzer's book that the more we use our
hippocampus, the better the hippocampus

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.860
becomes, the more efficient it becomes.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:54.150
For example, a violinist who plays
violin will develop parts of the brain

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:56.040
related to the movement of the fingers.

00:10:56.069 --> 00:11:00.870
The London cabbie develops parts of the brain
related to finding things on the map.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:04.490
If we have had a lot of experience
in learning languages, we are going

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:06.260
to be better at learning languages.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:07.910
Practice makes perfect.

00:11:07.939 --> 00:11:12.230
That includes, by the way, speaking. As
much as I'm a proponent of input-based

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.430
learning, because I know how effective
it is (listening and reading) in terms of

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:20.360
building up your sense of the language,
your sense of how the language works,

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:22.460
your vocabulary, your comprehension.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:26.030
By the same token, in order to
speak well, you have to speak a lot.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:26.960
Practice makes

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:27.720
perfect.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:33.810
So the sort of evolution of the attitude
is an area where the adult learner,

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:37.200
I think, and this is part of what
we've done with LingQ, where LingQ is

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.190
primarily aimed at adult learners,
learners who have a strategy, a

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.260
learning strategy, who have interests.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:47.910
So we try to allow the learner to
bring in content of interest as much as

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.860
possible from YouTube, from the internet.

00:11:49.985 --> 00:11:53.285
To maintain that interest,
maintain a positive attitude

00:11:53.315 --> 00:11:54.635
towards the learning experience.

00:11:54.695 --> 00:11:58.625
We try to provide encouragement in
the statistics: "You're doing well!"

00:11:58.685 --> 00:12:01.205
Obviously it's important to
reward people in what they do.

00:12:01.235 --> 00:12:05.375
Even with animals, if they feed the rat
after it runs around the maze, if there's

00:12:05.375 --> 00:12:07.655
reward, then there's a desire to learn.

00:12:07.805 --> 00:12:08.645
So reward

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:12.310
can help people learn. But the
reward needn't be an absolute reward.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:12.910
Like money.

00:12:12.939 --> 00:12:16.510
All it needs to be is an experience
that was better than expected.

00:12:16.569 --> 00:12:22.780
And I think there is another place where
adult learners need to avoid unrealistic

00:12:22.785 --> 00:12:25.449
expectations of how well they're going to do.

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:25.715
And,

00:12:25.715 --> 00:12:27.905
unfortunately, the
language books, they push,

00:12:27.905 --> 00:12:29.345
you know, here's this book, Assimil.

00:12:29.345 --> 00:12:32.825
You do this book, you're going to be at B2
in Persian, which is not the case.

00:12:32.975 --> 00:12:34.145
We need to be realistic.

00:12:34.175 --> 00:12:36.095
It's a long-term process.

00:12:36.125 --> 00:12:37.805
We want to enjoy the process.

00:12:37.835 --> 00:12:40.415
Again, when we speak, we're
going to speak with mistakes.

00:12:40.685 --> 00:12:43.265
We're going to make mistakes.
We're going to forget. If we have

00:12:43.265 --> 00:12:45.215
unrealistic expectations,

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:46.920
then we will be discouraged.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.580
If on the other hand, we don't have
unrealistic expectations, and then

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:55.950
we do well, then it's an unexpected,
pleasant result and that encourages us.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:00.780
So there are a number of ways in which
this attitude, which probably is to

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.970
the disadvantage of the adult learner.

00:13:03.449 --> 00:13:08.069
Just as the adult learners experience
can be an advantage compared to

00:13:08.069 --> 00:13:12.569
the child learning, the attitude is
often a disadvantage where the child

00:13:12.574 --> 00:13:17.910
is fearless, is playing, everything
is fun, and the adult is reluctant

00:13:17.939 --> 00:13:20.010
to be exposed, to make mistakes.

00:13:20.189 --> 00:13:25.500
I think that's an area where adults
can work harder on having a positive

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:30.030
attitude so that they're better able to
focus, better able to stay with the task.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.770
And if they do that, they will learn.

00:13:32.005 --> 00:13:36.595
And even if the adult cannot achieve
the same level of native-like

00:13:36.595 --> 00:13:40.465
pronunciation as the child, there's
all kinds of studies that show that

00:13:40.465 --> 00:13:46.435
the adult can accumulate a much larger
vocabulary in a much shorter period

00:13:46.435 --> 00:13:50.875
of time than a child can, and in many
cases, even than a native speaker has.

00:13:50.935 --> 00:13:52.975
The function of the brain is the same.

00:13:53.035 --> 00:13:54.025
The brain is there

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:58.829
throughout our lives. And it enables us
to deal with the world that we live in.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.760
It's a representation of the world that
we live in, but insofar as language

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:07.740
learning is concerned, while the process
of learning is fundamentally the same,

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.470
whether it's a baby or an adult.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:15.625
There are advantages that the baby has
in terms of its flexibility and its lack

00:14:15.625 --> 00:14:17.965
of impulse control, but lack of fear.

00:14:18.025 --> 00:14:21.685
But there are also advantages that the
adult has in terms of prior knowledge,

00:14:21.715 --> 00:14:26.655
ability to evolve a strategy, ability
to use reading, and other activities

00:14:27.065 --> 00:14:31.295
to improve in the language. Even if
there will be areas where the baby,

